        <h3>When budgets grow and cities still fail</h3>
        <p>India's municipal budgets have swelled over the past decade. Ban=
galore's civic body announced a budget of Rs 19,927 crore for 2025=E2=80=93=
26, up nearly 52%. Yet in January 2026, contaminated water from a sewage le=
ak in Indore killed at least 10 people and hospitalised more than 270. Writ=
ing for Scroll.in, sociologist Ankush Pal argues Indian cities are not fail=
ing because of a lack of resources, but because the political economy of ur=
ban governance rewards visibility over viability. Public money flows toward=
s projects that can be inaugurated and photographed =E2=80=94 metros, smart=
 city initiatives =E2=80=94 while the mundane work of maintaining sewage li=
nes and water systems goes unfunded.</p>
