        <h3>The conditions that make citizen participation in cities last: =
lessons from Brazil</h3>
        <p>In 1990, Porto Alegre in Brazil did something radical: it handed=
 citizens direct control over how a portion of the municipal budget would b=
e spent. The participatory budgeting process helped redistribute urban infr=
astructure to under-served communities. The model spread to more than 2,700=
 governments worldwide.</p>
        <p>A WRI case study examines what made it work, and why it eventual=
ly faltered. Political commitment, adequate financial resources, and well-s=
tructured participatory arrangements were the conditions that sustained it.=
 In India, where ward committees remain inactive in 78% of cities, the ques=
tion is not whether such participation is possible, but what it would take =
to make it last.</p>
