    <div class=3D"letter reveal visible">
      <p>According to <em>Municipal Performance of Indian Cities (2024)</em=
>, a joint report by the Institute for Competitiveness, Quality Council of =
India, and National Institute of Urban Affairs, this gap reflects a structu=
ral weakness in how India resources its cities. Over 50% of municipal corpo=
rations generate less than half their revenue independently, and most of wh=
at cities raise comes from property tax =E2=80=94 yet property valuations i=
n many cities have not been revised in years.</p>

      <p>The response, increasingly, has been borrowing. According to the R=
eserve Bank of India's 2024 Report on Municipal Finances, municipal borrowi=
ngs rose by 363% between 2019=E2=80=9320 and 2023=E2=80=9324, from Rs 2,886=
 crore to Rs 13,364 crore. But 81% of municipalities lack independent borro=
wing powers, requiring state approval for financial decisions =E2=80=94 cit=
ies are taking on more debt while retaining less control over how they mana=
ge it.</p>

      <p>What emerges is a compounding constraint: cities that cannot susta=
in themselves fiscally cannot plan independently, respond to local prioriti=
es, or hold themselves accountable to residents. The 74th Amendment envisio=
ned urban local bodies as institutions of self-government. The fiscal archi=
tecture required to make that vision real remains, three decades later, lar=
gely unbuilt.</p>

